                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/10877: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-06-05T12:00:00+08:00">June 5, 1930—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received
                        2:50 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>407. My 404, June 4, 6 p.m. Following from American Consul at Tsinanfu: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“June 4, 4 p.m. Shansi troops now are all along northern bank of Yellow
                            River and have been directing artillery and rifle fire since daylight
                            against Han’s men at Lokow, 5 miles north of Tsinanfu. A good many junks
                            including some loaded with cargoes of American oil burned and sunk. City
                            panicky as smoke of fires and boom of guns can be seen and heard. Shansi
                            columns will probably try to cross to the east and west of Tsinanfu with
                            the two railways as objectives. Am urging Americans here, particularly
                            women and children, to leave.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer>For the Minister:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >Perkins</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
